org】.\nL165: \nL166: 【47†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【48†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL167: \nL168: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【49†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL169: 【50†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【51†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【52†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【53†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【54†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【55†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【56†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【57†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL170: \nL171: Major funding support from\nL172: \nL173: 【58†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【59†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【60†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.2926)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.2926\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 369\nL0@P0: arXiv:1205.2926v2 [cs.SC] 25 Sep 2013\nL1@P0: FASTER ARITHMETIC FOR NUMBER-THEORETIC\nL2@P0: TRANSFORMS\nL3@P0: DAVID HARVEY\nL4@P0: Abstract. We show how to improve the efficiency of the computa\u0002tion of fast Fourier transforms over Fp where p is a word-sized prime.\nL5@P0: Our main technique is optimisation of the basic arithmetic, in effect de\u0002creasing the total number of reductions modulo p, by making use of a\nL6@P0: redundant representation for integers modulo p. We give performance\nL7@P0: results showing a significant improvement over Shoup’s NTL library.\nL8@P0: 1. Introduction\nL9@P0: An important problem in computational number theory and cryptography\nL10@P0: is the efficient implementation of modular arithmetic. A typical implemen\u0002tation strategy is to represent elements of Z/NZ by residues in a standard\nL11@P0: interval, such as [0, N) or [−N/2, N/2), and to reduce intermediate results\nL12@P0: to this interval after each operation in Z/NZ, such as addition or multipli\u0002cation.\nL13@P0: In many algorithms one can obtain a substantial constant factor speedup\nL14@P0: by delaying the modular reduction until after several arithmetic operations\nL15@P0: have been performed, by taking into account the bit-size of intermediate\nL16@P0: results. For example, to compute a dot product P\nL17@P0: i\nL18@P0: aibi, a fundamental\nL19@P0: operation in linear algebra, one may accumulate terms in batches, using or\u0002dinary integer (or even floating-point) arithmetic, and perform the reduction\nL20@P0: modulo N after each batch [1].\nL21@P0: The aim of this paper is to describe a strategy for reducing the number of\nL22@P0: modular reductions in the computation of a discrete Fourier transform over\nL23@P0: a finite field, also known as a number-theoretic transform (NTT). The NTT\nL24@P0: has a vast range of applications; we mention here only fast multiplication of\nL25@P0: large integers or polynomials [7].\nL26@P0: For simplicity we restrict to the following situation. Let β describe the\nL27@P0: machine word size, for example β = 232 or β = 264. We work over Fp =\nL28@P0: Z/pZ where p is a word-sized prime, and we assume that p = 1 (mod L),\nL29@P0: where L = 2ℓis the transform length. This ensures that Fp contains a\nL30@P0: primitive L-th root of unity; we fix one of these, denoted ω. The NTT is\nL31@P0: the map (Fp)\nL32@P0: L → (Fp)L defined by\nL33@P0: bj =\nL34@P0: L\nL35@P0: X−1\nL36@P0: i=0\nL37@P0: ω\nL38@P0: ijai\nL39@P0: , 0 ≤ j < L.\nL40@P0-1: 12 DAVID HARVEY\nL41@P1: Equivalently, this is the map that evaluates the polynomial a0 + a1x + · · · +\nL42@P1: aL−1x\nL43@P1: L−1 at the points 1, ω, ω2\nL44@P1: , . . . , ωL−1.\nL45@P1: It is well known that the fast Fourier transform (FFT) can be used to\nL46@P1: compute the NTT using O(Llog L) operations in Fp. For completeness, a\nL47@P1: simple in-place iterative radix-2 FFT algorithm is shown in Algorithm 1.\nL48@P1: Algorithm 1: Simple FFT\nL49@P1: Input: ω ∈ Fp and x = (x0, . . . , xL−1) ∈ (Fp)\nL50@P1: L, with L = 2ℓ\nL51@P1: Output: DFT of x with respect to ω, in bit-reversed order\nL52@P1: 1 for i ← 1, 2, . . . , ℓ do\nL53@P1: 2 ζ ← ω\nL54@P1: 2\nL55@P1: i−1\nL56@P1: 3 m ← 2\nL57@P1: ℓ−i\nL58@P1: 4 for 0 ≤ j < 2\nL59@P1: i−1 do\nL60@P1: 5 t ← 2jm\nL61@P1: 6 for 0 ≤ k < m do\nL62@P1: 7\nL63@P1: \u0014\nL64@P1: xt+k\nL65@P1: xt+k+m\nL66@P1: \u0015\nL67@P1: ←\nL68@P1: \u0014\nL69@P1: xt+k + xt+k+m\nL70@P1: ζ\nL71@P1: k\nL72@P1: (xt+k − xt+k+m)\nL73@P1: \u0015\nL74@P1: 8 end\nL75@P1: 9 end\nL76@P1: 10 end\nL77@P1: Our main focus in this paper is on the butterfly operation in line 7, which\nL78@P1: computes (X, Y ) 7→ (X +Y, W(X −Y )) for some fixed root of unity W ∈ Fp\nL79@P1: (a suitable power of ω). At first glance this requires one modular addition,\nL80@P1: one modular subtraction, and one modular multiplication, and this is how\nL81@P1: the butterfly is usually implemented. Of course these modular operations\nL82@P1: are themselves implemented on modern microprocessors using more basic\nL83@P1: primitives. For example, a modular addition is usually implemented as an\nL84@P1: ordinary integer addition, followed by a comparison with p, followed by a\nL85@P1: conditional subtraction. In this paper we investigate the butterfly at this\nL86@P1: lower level, showing how to streamline the implementation to reduce the\nL87@P1: number of comparisons and conditional operations. Another interpretation\nL88@P1: is that we have reduced the number of modular reductions.\nL89@P1: 2. A typical butterfly implementation\nL90@P1: Victor Shoup’s NTL (Number Theory Library) [6] is a popular C++ li\u0002brary used in computational number theory. It makes heavy use of the NTT.\nL91@P1: Its implementation of the butterfly (X, Y ) 7→ (X + Y, W(X − Y )) may be\nL92@P1: expressed by the pseudocode shown in Algorithm 2. It has been simplified to\nL93@P1: focus attention on the arithmetic aspects, ignoring issues like loop unrolling,\nL94@P1-2: software pipelining, and locality. All variables represent register-sized quan\u0002tities.FASTER ARITHMETIC FOR NUMBER-THEORETIC TRANSFORMS 3\nL95@P2: Algorithm 2: NTL butterfly implementation\nL96@P2: Input: p < β/2\nL97@P2: 0 < W < p\nL98@P2: W′ = ⌊W β/p⌋, 0 < W′ < β\nL99@P2: 0 ≤ X < p\nL100@P2: 0 ≤ Y < p\nL101@P2: Output: X′ = X + Y (mod p)\nL102@P2: Y\nL103@P2: ′ = W(X − Y ) (mod p)\nL104@P2: 1 X′ ← X + Y\nL105@P2: 2 if X′ ≥ p then X′ ← X′ − p\nL106@P2: 3 T ← X − Y\nL107@P2: 4 if T < 0 then T ← T + p\nL108@P2: 5 Q ← ⌊W′T /β⌋\nL109@P2: 6 Y\nL110@P2: ′ ← (W T − Qp) mod β\nL111@P2: 7 if Y\nL112@P2: ′ ≥ p then Y′ ← Y′ − p\nL113@P2: 8 return X′, Y ′\nL114@P2: Note that W and W′ are independent of the data being transformed; they\nL115@P2: can be precomputed and reused for each transform. A similar comment\nL116@P2: applies to all the butterfly algorithms considered in this paper.\nL117@P2: Theorem 1. Algorithm 2 is correct.\nL118@P2: Proof. Lines 1–2 compute the sum X + Y and reduce it modulo p to the\nL119@P2: standard interval [0, p), using the assumption p < β/2 to avoid overflow in\nL120@P2: the first line. Lines 3–4 compute T = X − Y (mod p) in the same way,\nL121@P2: assuming that T has a signed type for the comparison.\nL122@P2: Lines 5–7 compute the product W T (mod p). Line 5 first generates an\nL123@P2: estimated quotient Q. By the definition of W′ and Q we have\nL124@P2: 0 ≤\nL125@P2: W β\nL126@P2: p\nL127@P2: − W′ < 1, 0 ≤\nL128@P2: W′T\nL129@P2: β\nL130@P2: − Q < 1.\nL131@P2: Multiplying by T p/β and p respectively, and adding, yields\nL132@P2: 0 ≤ W T − Qp < T p\nL133@P2: β\nL134@P2: + p < 2p < β.\nL135@P2: In particular, line 6 correctly computes Y\nL136@P2: ′ = W T − Qp, and the single\nL137@P2: correction in line 7 suffices to reduce it into [0, p). \u0003\nL138@P2: The operations performed by Algorithm 2 map comfortably onto modern\nL139@P2: instruction sets. In line 5, the expression ⌊W′T /β⌋ represents the high word\nL140@P2: of the product W′T, which can typically be obtained by a single hardware\nL141@P2: multiply instruction. Both W T and Qp in line 6 require only the low word\nL142@P2-3: of the product. The conditional additions and subtractions in lines 2, 44 DAVID HARVEY\nL143@P3: and 7 are simple enough that a modern compiler will implement them by a\nL144@P3: conditional move instruction rather than by a branch.\nL145@P3: If we assume that X and Y are distributed uniformly in [0, p), then the\nL146@P3: first two conditions (lines 2 and 4) hold with probability 50%. The behaviour\nL147@P3: of the third condition (line 7) is more complex. If the quantity W β/p − W′\nL148@P3: considered in the proof of the theorem happens to be close to zero, then the\nL149@P3: condition will be satisfied with very low probability. At the other extreme,\nL150@P3: if W β/p−W′is close to unity, and if we assume that p is near β/2, then one\nL151@P3: can show the condition is satisfied with probability about 25%. In this case\nL152@P3: it is still reasonable to prefer a conditional move instruction to a branch.\nL153@P3: The modular multiplication algorithm in lines 5–7 is attributed to Shoup\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - nict-sfl/FasterNTT · GitHub (https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 356\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       